\label{sec:decoupling}

\input{tables/tab2_decoupling}

Despite the 4.4$\times$ peak reward gap, GRPO barely moves accuracy: all fine-grained methods at 20\% sparsity change by $|\Delta| < 1$\,pp (CKA-only $+$0.48, Standard $+$0.55, Random $-$0.53, \rasp{} $-$0.61\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:decoupling}).
We term this \emph{reward-accuracy decoupling}: reward trajectory reflects the model's capacity to learn from RL signal, not downstream accuracy (mean $|\Delta| {=} 0.54$\,pp, max $1.14$\,pp on GSM8K across all fine-grained criteria and sparsity levels).

The degree of decoupling varies with sparsity.
At 15\% (single seed), GRPO slightly amplifies the CKA-only advantage: the SFT gap of 5.69\,pp (72.18\% vs 66.49\%) widens to 7.20\,pp under GRPO (72.76\% vs 65.56\%).
At 20\%, GRPO adds nothing: the SFT gap of 4.62\,pp is preserved almost exactly (4.55\,pp).
The most extreme case is 25\%, where Standard's peak reward collapses to 0.008 yet accuracy still changes by only $+$0.99\,pp (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_sparsity}).
Across all fine-grained criteria and both sparsity levels (20\% and 25\%), $|\Delta| < 1$\,pp without exception on GSM8K (Tables~\ref{tab:decoupling},~\ref{tab:cross_sparsity}); on MATH-500, differences remain within statistical noise ($|\Delta| < 2.5$\,pp with stderr ${\approx}$2\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500}).
Moderate structural damage (15\%) apparently preserves enough capacity for GRPO to differentially benefit CKA-only pruning, while more aggressive pruning (20--25\%) exceeds the threshold where GRPO can improve upon SFT baselines.

These results are robust: CKA-only GRPO accuracy varies by only 0.36\,pp across three seeds (64.22--64.58\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_multiseed}), and extending training to 300 steps leaves accuracy unchanged (CKA-only 64.37\%$\to$64.37\%, Standard 59.44\%$\to$59.29\%).
The ranking and decoupling generalize beyond GSM8K: on MATH-500~\citep{hendrycks2021math}, SFT accuracy follows the same ordering (CKA-only 26.8\% $>$ Standard 25.8\% $>$ \rasp{} 21.2\% $>$ Random 14.4\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500}), and on MBPP~\citep{austin2021mbpp} all fine-grained GRPO $|\Delta| < 2$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_mbpp}).
\label{sec:ood}
ShortGPT~\citep{shortgpt2024} depth pruning is the sole exception: its GRPO delta is task-dependent ($-$1.36\,pp on GSM8K, $+$4.01\,pp on MBPP; Tables~\ref{tab:decoupling},~\ref{tab:mbpp}), unlike the uniformly small deltas of fine-grained methods ($|\Delta| \leq 1.34$\,pp, with the cross-benchmark maximum from MBPP).


\subsection{Additional Results}
\label{sec:additional_results}

\paragraph{Post-SFT accuracy does not predict GRPO outcome.}
\label{sec:sft_gradient}
Under SFT alone, CKA-only (63.91\%) $>$ \rasp{} (61.49\%) $>$ Standard (59.29\%) $>$ Random (51.25\%), yet under GRPO the \rasp{}/Standard ordering reverses (peak 0.081 vs 0.061) and Random outperforms both in peak reward (0.190).
More strikingly, ShortGPT post-SFT (44.73\%) shows GRPO actively harming accuracy ($-$1.36\,pp), while unstructured pruning achieves the highest post-SFT accuracy (87.87\%) with GRPO accuracy 87.64\% ($\Delta{=}{-}0.23$\,pp), confirming that pruning criteria are irrelevant when structural damage is minimal (96.5\% dense retention).
Post-SFT accuracy does not predict GRPO recovery outcome: the spectrum is driven by structural properties of the pruned architecture.

\paragraph{Sparsity limits and dose-response.}
\label{sec:sparsity_cliff}
All methods collapse at 30\% sparsity, validating 20\% as the operating point (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sparsity_cliff}).
Pruning reduces parameters by 19\% and memory by ${\sim}$28\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_inference}).
On GSM8K, the CKA coefficient $\alpha$ exhibits an inverted-U pattern in recovery accuracy across $\{0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0\}$, peaking at $\alpha{=}2.0$ (SFT 64.97\%, GRPO 66.11\%) with $|\Delta| \leq 1.14$\,pp at every point; $\alpha{=}1.0$ collapses (peak reward 0.061) while $\alpha{=}4.0$ recovers to 62.77\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_alpha}).


\subsection{Extended Analysis}
\label{sec:extended_analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig4_cka_heatmap.pdf}
    \caption{Layer-wise CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations (28 layers). CKA-only diverges most from dense ($\mu{=}0.62$) yet recovers best; Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) preserve dense fidelity but collapse under RL.}
    \label{fig:cka_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\input{tables/tab5_cross_scale}

\paragraph{Representational diversity.}
CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_heatmap}) shows CKA-only diverges most from the dense model ($\mu{=}0.62$), while Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) cluster near 1.0.
Preserving dense fidelity through gradient importance appears to retain redundant representations at the expense of the diversity that GRPO exploration requires.
CKA-only concentrates pruning in early layers (L0--9, 72.5\% of removed groups) while Standard prunes uniformly, removing late-layer structures that are representationally unique (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_overlap}).
Gradient flow analysis corroborates this: CKA-only models exhibit 2.89$\times$ mean gradient magnitude versus Standard across 55/56 layer groups ($p{<}0.05$, Wilcoxon; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_gradient}), consistent with diversity preservation enabling stronger RL training signals.

\paragraph{Extended training cannot rescue collapse.}
Extending GRPO from 100 to 300 steps (Figure~\ref{fig:extended_training}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_extended}), Standard shows zero net improvement (reward 0.054--0.058, $f_0 > 0.5$ throughout), while CKA-only sustains a 5.5$\times$ gap (0.308 vs 0.056 at step 300). The gap is structural, not a matter of training budget.

\paragraph{Cross-scale and cross-architecture generalization.}
On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B at 20\% sparsity (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale}), the three-method ordering is preserved (CKA-only 35.48\% $\gg$ \rasp{} 26.16\% $>$ Standard 22.29\%), with reward-accuracy decoupling confirmed at this scale ($|\Delta| < 1$\,pp: CKA-only $-$0.15, Standard $-$0.61\,pp).
On Qwen2.5-Math-7B at 20\% sparsity (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_arch}), CKA-only causes near-total initial damage (1.0\% vs Standard's 18.5\%) yet SFT recovers it to 51.50\% $\pm$ 3.53 while Standard remains at 21.00\% $\pm$ 2.88 ($N{=}200$)---recoverability depends on preserved structure, not starting accuracy. GRPO validation on this architecture is left to future work.

